## The idea

Values alone lose the shape, so also write a marker **"#" for every missing child**. Write the tree level by level with a queue; read it back with the same queue — each node in the queue takes the next two items as its children.

## Step by step

1. Serialize: BFS; write a value for a node, "#" for null; queue both children even when null.
2. Drop trailing "#"s.
3. Deserialize: first item is the root; for each node taken from the queue, the next two items are its left and right ("#" = none).

## Worked example

**Input:** tree [1, 2, 3, null, null, 4, 5]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| write | "1,2,3,#,#,4,5" |
| read 1 | children 2 and 3 |
| read 2 | children #, # → none |
| read 3 | children 4 and 5 |

**Result:** the same tree, rebuilt exactly

## Cost

O(n) time; the string is O(n). Iterative, so a 100,000-deep tree is fine.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

